      "rag": {
        "question": "How is the update gate implemented in a GRU?",
        "depth": "normal",
        "work_id": "AI-CSE-III-I-GRU-NOTES",
        "answer": "The update gate \\( z_t \\) in a GRU implements a decision-making mechanism:\n\n1. It uses a sigmoid function to determine how much of the previous hidden state (\\( h_{t-1} \\)) should be retained for the next time step.\n   \n   - Equation: \\( z_{t} = \\sigma(W_z \\cdot [h_{t}, x_{t}] + b_z) \\)\n   \n   Here, \\( W_z \\) represents weight matrix, \\( h_{i} \\) denotes the hidden states, \\( x_{j} \\)'s represent inputs, and \\( b_z \\)' s are biases. \n\n2. The sigmoid function outputs a value between 0 and 1, where:\n   - Values close to 0 indicate little retention of the old state.\n   - A value near 1 retains almost the entire old state.\n\n3. After obtaining \\( z \\), the equation for the new hidden state \\( h_t \\):\n   - \\( h'_t = tanh(W_h \\cdot [\\tilde{r}_t, h_{\\text{new}}] + b_h) \\)\n\n4. Then, combining the updated hidden state with the previous state via the update gate:\n   \\( h^{'}_t = (1 - z_t) \\cdot h_{(\\text{old})} + z_t * h'_{\\text{t}} \\)\n\nSo, the update rule updates the hidden state considering both the old hidden state and the newly computed candidate hidden state, influenced by the update_gate \\( z^{}",
